PRIVATE REFLECTION: {Majority (now ~18/20) continues to converge on E under LSO r. 3.3-3: Eric’s explicit “bought gun…tonight…end things for both of us” supplies both reasonable belief and imminence; disclosure of names/addresses/identifiers/locations was limited to what police requested and plausibly needed for same-day location of a mobile client, satisfying the “reasonably necessary” commentary. Minority (Colleagues 11, 18) still selects C on over-disclosure grounds (Florida addresses and children’s identities exceed strict necessity). 

A/B/D remain uniformly rejected (imminence test met; safety exception exists; police request alone creates no duty). 

Scope disagreement is the sole live issue; arguments now emphasize that all items were tied to real-time prevention and police request rather than gratuitous. My prior view (E) is reinforced; over-disclosure objection addressed but not eliminated by practical-context reading of the rule. No new rule text or facts introduced.}

